# ============================================================
# Hit Your Mark Travel — line-ending policy
# ============================================================
#
# Source is stored AND checked out as LF, on every platform.
#
# core.autocrlf is true on the machine this repo is worked on. It converts on
# checkout but not when a tool writes a file, so source files ended up carrying
# whichever ending last touched them: CRLF where a checkout wrote them, LF
# where one of the tools/ scripts did. Astro inlines source bytes into the
# built HTML, so that split leaked straight through into dist/ — 123 of the
# 133 committed dist files carry MIXED endings, and each rebuild flipped
# whichever subset had been rewritten since the last one. 82 such files landed
# in one PR that changed a single page.
#
# It is not only noise. public/.htaccess is checked out CRLF while the copy
# committed to dist/ has 11 LF lines, so `npm run verify` fails on a clean
# checkout with "dist/.htaccess differs from public/.htaccess" — two files that
# are byte-identical apart from line endings.
#
# eol=lf makes the working tree byte-identical to the blob everywhere, so the
# build has one input and therefore one output. text=auto still auto-detects
# binaries and leaves them alone.
* text=auto eol=lf

# dist/ is build output. `astro build` writes it and the deploy uploads it to
# Hostinger byte-for-byte, so Git must not rewrite its line endings. This rule
# is LAST on purpose: the last matching pattern wins, so -text overrides the
# eol=lf above for these paths. What the build writes is what gets stored, and
# what gets stored is what gets checked out.
#
# Keeping dist/ raw is only safe once the build's inputs are stable, which is
# what the rule above is for.
dist/** -text
